---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BodyFatCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/BodyFatCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="body-fat-calculator">
  <BodyFatCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to measure yourself properly</h2>
    <p>
      The result of the US Navy method depends on the tape measurements. Five minutes of care here
      improve the result more than any change to the equation.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Use a flexible tape that does not stretch. A cloth tailor's tape is ideal. A metal builder's
        tape does not follow the curves of the body.
      </li>
      <li>
        Measure early in the morning, after the bathroom and before eating or drinking. Food and fluid
        change waist circumference by a centimetre or more.
      </li>
      <li>
        Measure on bare skin, with the tape snug but not compressing. If it leaves a mark, it is too
        tight. If it sags, it is too loose.
      </li>
      <li>
        Take each measurement three times and use the average. Put the tape on again between attempts;
        do not read it twice in the same position.
      </li>
      <li>Stand relaxed, arms at your sides, breathing normally. Do not pull in your stomach.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Where each measurement goes</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Site</th>
            <th scope="col">Placement</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Neck</td>
            <td>
              Just below the larynx, tape sloping slightly downward toward the front. Do not include
              the shoulders.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Waist (men)</td>
            <td>Horizontally at the navel, at the end of a normal breath out.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Waist (women)</td>
            <td>At the narrowest point of the torso, usually a little above the navel.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Hips (women)</td>
            <td>At the widest point of the buttocks, feet together, tape level all the way round.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The two methods on this page</h2>
    <h3>US Navy circumference method</h3>
    <p>
      The Naval Health Research Center developed this method in 1984 as a practical field standard. It
      uses the logarithm of a circumference difference, which captures how fat is distributed around
      the trunk:
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Men:</strong> %BF = 495 ÷ (1.0324 − 0.19077 × log₁₀(waist − neck) + 0.15456 × log₁₀(height))
      − 450
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Women:</strong> %BF = 495 ÷ (1.29579 − 0.35004 × log₁₀(waist + hip − neck) + 0.22100 ×
      log₁₀(height)) − 450
    </p>
    <p>
      All measurements are in centimetres. Imperial entries are converted before the logarithms, so
      both unit systems give the same results.
    </p>

    <h3>BMI method (Deurenberg equation)</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>%BF = (1.20 × BMI) + (0.23 × age) − (10.8 × sex) − 5.4</strong>, where sex is 1 for men
      and 0 for women. Deurenberg and colleagues published it in 1991. It needs no tape, but it uses
      only height, weight, age and sex, so it cannot tell muscle from fat. This page shows it as a
      cross-check, not as the main figure.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the two disagree, the gap tells you something. If the Navy figure is lower, you have more
      muscle than average for your weight. If it is higher, you have more fat than your BMI suggests:
      the "normal weight obesity" pattern.
    </p>

    <h2>How the methods compare on accuracy</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Method</th>
            <th scope="col">Typical error vs DEXA</th>
            <th scope="col">Cost and practicality</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>DEXA scan</td><td>Reference standard (±1–2%)</td><td>Clinic visit, £50–150 per scan</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hydrostatic weighing</td><td>±2%</td><td>Specialist lab, uncomfortable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Air displacement plethysmography</td><td>±2–3%</td><td>Specialist lab</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Skinfold callipers (7-site)</td><td>±3–4% with a skilled tester</td><td>Cheap, but depends on technique</td></tr>
          <tr><td>US Navy tape method</td><td>±3–4%</td><td>Free, two minutes, repeatable at home</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bioimpedance scales</td><td>±4–8%, very sensitive to hydration</td><td>Cheap and instant, but drifts with fluid status</td></tr>
          <tr><td>BMI-based estimate</td><td>±5–8%, worse for athletes</td><td>Needs no equipment</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The tape method is about as accurate as equipment that costs hundreds of times more. Its main
      strength is repeatability: measured the same way every two weeks, it tracks change accurately,
      even if the absolute number is a few points off.
    </p>

    <h2>Body fat categories</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Category</th>
            <th scope="col">Men</th>
            <th scope="col">Women</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Essential fat</td><td>2–5%</td><td>10–13%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Athletes</td><td>6–13%</td><td>14–20%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fitness</td><td>14–17%</td><td>21–24%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Average</td><td>18–24%</td><td>25–31%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Obese</td><td>25% and above</td><td>32% and above</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      These are the bands of the American Council on Exercise. Women have more essential fat than men
      for reproductive and hormonal function, so each threshold is about 8 to 10 points higher. Very
      low body fat over a long time (below about 5 percent in men or 12 percent in women) disrupts
      hormones, bone density and immune function. Do not aim for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Waist-to-height ratio: the two-second version</h2>
    <p>
      If you measure only one thing, measure your waist and divide it by your height. Keep it below 0.5.
      This one ratio predicts cardiometabolic risk at least as well as BMI, works across heights and
      populations, and needs no equations. It shows next to your body fat results above.
    </p>

    <h2>Tracking change over time</h2>
    <p>
      Body fat changes slowly. A realistic rate of loss is 0.5 to 1 percentage point a month with
      training and a modest calorie deficit. Measure every two to four weeks, always under the same
      conditions, and compare the trend, not single readings. A tape measure, progress photos and a
      lifting log tell you more about body composition than one scan.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
